o GDP+1%; **no company guidance exists beyond 2026** \u2014 flagged per framework]\n- Margins: [Assumption \u2014 services mix + price-cost; could be upset by EMEA margin drag (Middle East -30% revenue)]\n\n## 3-3. Terminal Value\n\n```\nTerminal FCF = $4.65B \u00d7 1.03 = $4.79B\nTerminal growth = 3.0% [Assumption \u2014 long-run nominal GDP, <3.5% discount]\nWACC = 9.4%\nTerminal Value = $4.79B / (9.4% - 3.0%) = $74.8B [Estimate]\n```\n\n## 3-4. DCF Calculation\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| PV FCFs 2026-2030 (9.4%) | $13.63B |\n| PV Terminal Value | $47.75B |\n| EV | $61.38B |\n| Less: Net debt (LT debt 3.92 + current portion 0.69 + leases 1.7 - cash 1.76) | -$4.55B |\n| Equity value | $56.83B |\n| Per share (220.3M shares) | **$258** |\n\n**Sensitivity \u2014 WACC \u00d7 Terminal growth:**\n\n| WACC | g=2.5% | g=3.0% | g=3.5% |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 8.4% | $320 | $350 | $389 |\n| 9.4% | $260 | $281 | $309 |\n| 10.4% | $214 | $229 | $248 |\n\n## 3-5. Multiples Cross-Check\n\n| Metric | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Trailing P/E (TTM NI $2.949B, 220.3M sh) | 33.8x |\n| Forward P/E (FY26 adj EPS $15.25) | 29.7x |\n| EV/EBITDA (estimated FY26) | ~22x |\n| P/FCF (FY25 FCF $2.84B) | 35x |\n| 10-yr median P/E (estimated) | ~25x [Estimate \u2014 not directly in pack] |\n| Peer set (Carrier ~28x fwd, JCI ~20x, Daikin ~25x) | TT is 10-20% above peer median [Estimate \u2014 web-derived] |\n\n**Multiples indicate**: TT is priced at a ~20% premium to quality peers and a ~30% premium to its own history.\n\n## 3-6. Reverse DCF \u2014 What does $453.43 imply?\n\nTo justify $453.43 at 9.4% WACC and 3% terminal growth, the market is embedding:\n- FY2027 revenue: $26.5B (+15%, above guidance momentum)\n- FY2030 revenue: $42B (7-year CAGR ~12%)\n- FY2030 FCF margin: 17% \u2192 FCF \u2248 $7.1B\n- OR a lower required return (~7.5-8.0%) \u2014 implying the market treats TT as a bond-proxy quality compounder.\n\n**Verdict: FEASIBLE only in the bull case; EXCESSIVE relative to base-rate evidence.**\n\n## 3-7. Tornado Sensitivity \u2014 Top 3 Variables\n\n1. **FY2027-29 revenue growth (\u00b14%)**: \u00b1$65/share impact \u2014 **Thesis KPI: Bookings and backlog conversion**\n2. **WACC (\u00b1100bps)**: \u00b1$50/share \u2014 **Thesis KPI: 10Y Treasury and beta**\n3. **Terminal margin (\u00b1150bps)**: \u00b1$35/share \u2014 **Thesis KPI: Services mix / price-cost**\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\n## 3.5-1. Competitive Response\n\nCarrier, JCI, Daikin, Mitsubishi, and Vertiv are all expanding data-center cooling capacity. If TT's success continues, expect:\n- **Price competition** in liquid cooling (-2 impact): Vertiv has strong position, LiquidStack acquisition helps TT.\n- **Capacity response** (-1): Competitors will also add 2-3x capacity; segment won't stay supply-constrained.\n- **Net effect: -2** (mild-to-moderate headwind after 2027)\n\n## 3.5-2. Reflexivity Check (Soros)\n\n- **Positive**: TT's high P/E gives it cheap acquisition currency (Stellar), high SBC attracts AI talent, backlog reinforces customer trust.\n- **Negative**: If the stock corrects 30%, SBC becomes less valuable, customers may delay decisions, and the \"quality premium\" narrative could reverse.\n- **Net: Positive while the stock is high \u2014 but this cuts both ways on the downside. High crowding makes the reflexivity risk asymmetric.**\n\n## 3.5-3. Customer Behavior Evolution\n\n- Early adopters: Hyperscalers (2019-2023) \u2192 Mainstream: Colocation and enterprise data centers (2024-2026) \u2192 Late: Commercial buildings with AI-driven efficiency.\n- LTV/CAC: TT's service attach rate on new chillers is high; services CVaR is stable.\n- Risk: Hyperscalers are sophisticated buyers with pricing power; as they standardize designs, they may commoditize the hardware and squeeze margins (already visible in the \"four $100M+ orders\" \u2014 these are large, competitive bids).\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\n## 4-1/4-2. Scenario Composition\n\n| Sc