ial HVAC supportive, residential weak)\n- Commodity sensitivity: \u22121 (copper, steel, refrigerant costs)\n\nStep 1-4. Macro alignment\nExpected macro impact: slight net headwind (\u22120.5) \u2014 Reflation regime favors value/cyclicals over a 30x-multiple compounder; rising 10Y pressures long-duration multiples.\n- Conviction: \u22120.5 step. Action: strengthen split-exit discipline on any strength.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1.5 \u2014 BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\nStep 1.5-1. Base rates\n- Fortune 500-quality industrial compounder surviving 5 years: ~95% [Assumption].\n- Mature industrial sustaining >8% organic growth for 10 years: ~25\u201335% [Assumption].\n- \"Applied bookings +130% for 4 consecutive quarters\" reverting to <+30% within 4 quarters: ~50% [Assumption - cyclical mean-reversion base rate].\n- Large-cap HVAC trading >30x forward EPS sustaining that multiple for 3+ years: ~40\u201360% in late-cycle [Assumption].\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate\nMy base case (9% \u2192 fade to 4% growth, 21% terminal OPM) is disciplined \u2014 it assumes a strong but not heroic outcome.\nThe market price requires deviation above base rate: it embeds ~6% perpetual growth at ~8.5% discount. Justification requires:\n1. $12.1B backlog +70% YoY, of which ~$6B is for 2027+ [Actual - company transcript];\n2. Services \u22481/3 of revenue compounding low-teens since 2020 (annuity quality) [Actual - company transcript];\n3. Regulatory tailwinds (efficiency standards, refrigerant phase-downs, decarbonization) mandate replacement demand [Actual - industry].\nThese justify a quality premium, but NOT a certainty premium for a 35x multiple.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base-rate shock check\nSuccess probability implied by my Base+Bull = 75%. Base-rate-adjusted \u2248 50%. Ratio = 1.5x \u2014 at the red-flag threshold. The three justifications above are real but cyclical mean-reversion in applied bookings is the dominant risk. Flag: the bull case must not be allowed to become the base case without new evidence.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY\n\nStep 2-1. Business understanding (3 sentences)\nTT designs, manufactures, and services HVAC systems (commercial applied chillers, residential units, controls) plus transport refrigeration. It monetizes an installed base through services (~1/3 of revenue, low-teens CAGR) and is expanding into data-center liquid cooling and AI-enabled building optimization. Demand is driven by energy efficiency regulation, electrification, and AI-infrastructure thermal management. Understandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat assessment (0\u20133 each)\n- Intangible assets (Trane/American Standard brand): 3\n- Switching costs (installed controls, service contracts, chiller-plant integration): 3\n- Network effects: 1 (service-density benefits, controls ecosystem \u2014 mild)\n- Cost advantage: 2 (scale manufacturing; not lowest cost)\n- Economies of scale (service footprint, distribution, R&D $348M): 3\n- Regulatory barriers (energy codes, refrigerant rules, building standards): 3\n- Data moat (building telemetry, agentic AI, BrainBox): 2\nTOTAL: 17/21 \u2192 FORTRESS (range 16\u201321). Direction: widening in services/AI; stable-to-narrowing in residential/commodity equipment.\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification\nFast Grower with cyclical exposure. Checkpoints: PEG = 29.7x forward / 14.6% growth \u2248 2.0 (rich); inventory+receivables growth tracked via backlog; debt low. A classic \"great growth company \u2014 but the price matters.\"\n\nStep 2-4. Financial strength scorecard\n- Revenue 3-yr CAGR (2022\u21922025): 21.322/15.992 = 10.1% [Arithmetic]; 5-yr CAGR +11.3% [pack].\n- Operating margin: FY25 18.6%; FY24 17.6%; FY23 16.4%; 12-yr median 12.5% [Arithmetic].\n- Net margin: FY25 13.7% [Arithmetic]. ROE: 36.6% [vendor].\n- ROIC (my calc): NOPAT 3.967\u00d70.79 = 3.13 [Estimate]; IC = equity 8.601 + debt (LT 3.922 + current 0.693 + leases 0.825) \u2212 cash 1.763 = 12.28 [Arithmetic]; ROIC \u2248 25.5% [Estimate].\n- FCF conversion: FY25 FCF 2.837 / NI 2.919 = 97%; owner-FCF (less SBC 0.087) = 2.750 = 94% [Arithmetic]. TTM OCF/NI = 3.912/2.949 = 133% [Arithmetic].\n- Net debt/EBITDA: 3.677 / (OI 3.967 + D&A 0.376) = 0.85x [Arithmetic/Estimate]. Interest coverage \u2248 17.5x [Arithmetic].\nVERDICT: EXCELLENT.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard\n1. Buyback efficiency: 2/3 (consistent \u22121.8%/yr count; $840M YTD 2026, but at ~30x trailing \u2014 expensive buybacks)\n2. M&A track record: 2/3 (Stellar revenue target $500M, EPS-neutral on reinvestment \u2014 unproven)\n3. Capex efficiency: 3/3 (incremental ROIC \u2248 54% [Estimate: \u0394NOPAT 2023\u219225 0.85 / \u0394IC 1.58])\n4. Rationality of mix: 3/3 (capacity + buyback + dividend 12% increase; dividend $4.20 = 32% payout [Arithmetic])\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3/3 (raised FY26 guidance twice in one year)\nInsider ownership 0.27% \u2014 no bonus; no recent insider cluster buy in pack.\nTOTAL: 13/15. EXCELLENT.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2.5 \u2014 INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\nStep 2.5-1. R&D capitalization\nR&D 2025: $348M [Actual - TT press release]. 5-year straight-line amortization.\nAdjusted OI = 3.967 + 0.348 \u2212 0.070 = $4.245B [Estimate].\nUnamortized R&D \u2248 $1.0B [Estimate]. Adjusted IC \u2248 13.28B [Estimate].\n\nStep 2.5-2. SBC treatment\nSBC FY25 = $0.087B [Actual - pack]. Owner-FCF = OCF 3.220 \u2212 capex 0.383 \u2212 SBC 0.087 = $2.750B [Arithmetic]. SBC is 0.4% of revenue \u2014 not a franchise issue.\n\nStep 2.5-3. Operating leases\nOperating leases FY25: $0.825B [Actual - pack]. Adjusted net debt = LT 3.922 + current portion 0.693 + leases 0.825 \u2212 cash 1.763 = $3.677B [Arithmetic]. (Vendor total-debt scalar $4.615B differs because of definition; I use the filed components.)\n\nStep 2.5-4/5. Adjusted ROIC\nAdjusted ROIC = (4.245 \u00d7 0.79) / 13.28 = 25.2% [Estimate]. GAAP ROIC \u2248 25.5%. Gap <1%p \u2014 immaterial. TT is an R&D-light industrial; GAAP already captures the economics.\n\nStep 2.5-6. Owner earnings (FY25)\nOwner Earnings = NI 2.919 + D&A 0.376 \u2212 Maintenance capex (\u2248 total 0.383) \u2212 \u0394WC 0.307 = $2.605B [Arithmetic].\nForward owner earnings power (FY26E): ~$3.1\u20133.3B [Estimate].\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (ENGINE 1: DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\nStep 0-1..0-4. Engine selection\nArchetype: B \u2014 Quality Compounder (not C: services annuity and backlog reduce cyclicality; not D: \"platform\" is a service business, not a product-platform network; not E: the data-center optionality is already converting to booked revenue).\nEngine 1 (DCF + Multiples). Alternative Engine 3 would not change the conclusion: the \"platform premium\" has no independent numeric basis in the pack.\nTransition check: TT is NOT transitioning archetypes. It stays B; the market is attaching an option premium, but the business model is fundamentals-driven.\n\nStep 3-1. Discount-rate derivation (WACC)\n- Risk-free (10Y): 4.71% [Actual - Trading Economics, 08/21/26]\n- ERP: 4.25% [Assumption \u2014 Damodaran implied range ~4.2\u20134.8; HY spreads benign at 2.75%, so mid-low]\n- Beta: 1.00 [Assumption \u2014 vendor beta 1.205 has unverified window; TT is lower-vol than that implies. Using 1.0 is defensible and not flattering]\n- Cost of equity = 4.71 + 1.00 \u00d7 4.25 = 8.96%\n- Pre-tax cost of debt: 5.0% [Assumption \u2014 A-rated industrial; credit rating [Unconfirmed]]\n- Tax rate: 21% [Assumption \u2014 statutory; TT's effective-rate inputs are provably broken in pack]\n- After-tax Kd = 3.95%\n- Weights: E = 99.891 / (99.891+3.677) = 96.4%; D = 3.6% [Arithmetic]\n- WACC = 0.964 \u00d7 8.96 + 0.036 \u00d7 3.95 = 8.78% \u2192 use 8.75% [Estimate]\n\nStep 3-2. Explicit forecast \u2014 BASE CASE (per-year, fully stated)\n[Actual] = guided/historical; [Estimate] = my forecast from backlog/industry data; [Assumption] = fade path.\n\n| Year | Revenue $B | Growth | OPM | Net margin | NI $B | D&A $B | SBC $B | \u0394WC $B | OCF $B | Capex $B | Owner-FCF $B |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2025A | 21.322 | +7.5% | 18.6% | 13.7% | 2.919 | 0.376 | 0.087 | +0.307 | 3.220 | 0.383 | 2.750 |\n| 2026E | 23.24 | +9.0% [Actual-guide] | 19.2% | 14.2% | 3.30 | 0.51 | 0.10 | +0.38 | 3.65 | 0.58 | 2.97 |\n| 2027E | 26.03 | +12.0% [Estimate] | 19.8% | 14.6% | 3.80 | 0.57 | 0.12 | +0.56 | 4.06 | 0.68 | 3.26 |\n| 2028E | 28.37 | +9.0% [Estimate] | 20.3% | 14.9% | 4.24 | 0.62 | 0.13 | +0.47 | 4.66 | 0.68 | 3.85 |\n| 2029E | 30.36 | +7.0% [Assumption] | 20.6% | 15.0% | 4.55 | 0.67 | 0.14 | +0.40 | 5.11 | 0.67 | 4.30 |\n| 2030E | 32.03 | +5.5% [Assumption] | 20.8% | 15.1% | 4.84 | 0.70 | 0.14 | +0.33 | 5.51 | 0.64 | 4.73 |\n| 2031E | 33.47 | +4.5% [Assumption] | 20.9% | 15.1% | 5.04 | 0.74 | 0.15 | +0.29 | 5.81 | 0.67 | 4.99 |\n| 2032E | 34.81 | +4.0% [Assumption] | 21.0% | 15.1% | 5.24 | 0.77 | 0.16 | +0.27 | 6.07 | 0.70 | 5.21 |\n\nDriver sourcing:\n- 2026 revenue +9% [Actual - company FY organic guidance]; Q2 actual organic +9%, Q3 guide ~10%.\n- 2027 +12% [Estimate - from $12.1B backlog +70% with ~$6B for 2027+, applied revenue already +40%, services low-teens; company has NOT given FY27 guidance \u2014 this is my conversion estimate].\n- 2028\u201332 fade to 4%: [Assumption] \u2014 after the guided period, no source exists; this is a deliberate fade to HVAC secular growth (~4\u20135%) plus gradual data-center normalization.\n- OPM: 19.2% \u2192 21.0% [Estimate/Assumption] \u2014 continuation of FY23\u219225 100bp/yr margin expansion (mix shift to services, price/cost); 21% cap held flat thereafter.\n- Capex: $0.58B 2026 = 2.5% of revenue [Actual - CFO: 2\u20133% of revenue, capacity expansion], normalizing to 2.0\u20132.2% [Assumption].\n- \u0394WC = 20% \u00d7 incremental revenue [Estimate - calibrated to FY24 actual 0.453/2.160 and FY25 actual 0.307/1.484].\n- OCF = NI + D&A + SBC \u2212 \u0394WC + other (0.5% revenue, calibrated to FY25 actual) [Estimate].\n- Owner-FCF = OCF \u2212 capex \u2212 SBC (deducting SBC as a real expense per pack guidance) [Arithmetic].\n\nTerminal: g = 3.0% [Assumption - below long-run nominal GDP; quality compounder terminal]. Terminal FCF 2033 = 5.21 \u00d7 1.03 = $5.37B [Arithmetic].\n\nStep 3-3. DCF (WACC 8.75%)\nPV of explicit FCFs (2026\u20132032):\n2.97/1.0875 = 2.73\n3.26/1.0875\u00b2 = 2.76\n3.85/1.0875\u00b3 = 2.99\n4.30/1.0875\u2074 = 3.08\n4.73/1.0875\u2075 = 3.11\n4.99/1.0875\u2076 = 3.02\n5.21/1.0875\u2077 = 2.90\nSum PV FCF = $20.58B [Estimate]\nTerminal value = 5.37 / (0.0875 \u2212 0.03) = $93.4B; PV = 93.4 / 1.0875\u2077 = $51.9B\nEnterprise value = $72.5B\n+ Cash 1.763 [Actual]\n\u2212 Debt (LT 3.922 + current portion 0.693 + operating leases 0.825) = 5.440 [Actual/Arithmetic]\n= Equity value = $68.8B\n\u00f7 220.3M shares = $312/share [Estimate]\n\nSensitivity to discount rate & terminal g (with all else fixed):\nWACC = 9.75% \u2192 IV $262 | 9.25% \u2192 $285 | 8.75% \u2192 $312 | 8.25% \u2192 $343\nTerminal g 2.5% \u2192 $292; 3.5% \u2192 $336\nCONCLUSION: Base IV \u2248 $312 (range $285\u2013$345).\n\nStep 3-4. Multiples cross-check\n- Trailing P/E: 35.7x [Actual vendor]; Forward P/E FY26E (adjusted 15.25) = 29.7x; FY27E (17.35) \u2248 26.1x.\n- EV/EBITDA: EV = 103.6 / EBITDA 4.34 = 23.9x trailing [Estimate].\n- PEG \u2248 2.0 (29.7 / 14.6). Historical TT forward multiple [Unconfirmed \u2014 could not retrieve reliable 10-yr series; from memory ~18\u201325x pre-2024, mark [Estimate] and low confidence].\nAt 25.0x FY27E EPS of $17.35 \u2192 $434. At 30x FY26E $15.25 \u2192 $458. The market is paying the top of TT's historical band.\n\nStep 3-5. Tornado \u2014 top-3 value drivers (Thesis KPIs)\n1. WACC / 10Y path: \u00b10.5% \u2192 \u00b1$28 (IV $285\u2013$343) \u2014 KPI: 10Y Treasury\n2. 2027\u201328 revenue conversion: \u00b12pts growth \u2192 \u00b1$20 \u2014 KPI: Applied bookings YoY, backlog conversion\n3. Terminal OPM: 21% vs 19.5% \u2192 \u00b1$15 \u2014 KPI: Segment mix/services growth\nAlso note: if WACC falls to 7.5% (market-implied), IV = ~$400 \u2014 see Reverse DCF below.\n\nStep 3-6. Reverse DCF (what the market believes)\nAt $453.43, the market implies either (a) a ~7.0\u20137.5% equity discount rate with my FCF path, or (b) 2032 owner-FCF \u2248 $8.5\u20139.0B / EPS \u2248 $38\u201340 (\u224815% EPS CAGR for a decade) \u2014 i.e., the data-center bull case becomes the BASE case. Feasible, but NOT verifiable from current disclosures.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\nStep 3.5-1. Competitive response\nIf TT's data-center applied strategy keeps winning (130% bookings growth, 4x capacity), Vertiv, Modine, Carrier, and JCI will expand aggressively; hyperscalers will multi-source and force price convergence. Nvidia's rack-architecture choices can reset the game (CES 2026 comments rattled cooling stocks \u2014 this event is [Actual]). Net impact: \u22121 (competition will compress applied margins over time).\n\nStep 3.5-2. Reflexivity\nPositive: high share price \u2192 cheap M&A currency (Stellar), customer confidence (critical for 5-year service contracts), talent attraction. Negative: a derating would have mild effects given SBC is only 0.4% of revenue. Reflexivity is positive but contained.\n\nStep 3.5-3. Customer behavior\nHyperscalers are sophisticated, procurement-driven buyers \u2014 switching costs are moderate in equipment, high in service/controls. As AI cooling moves from early adopters to mainstream, equipment margins will normalize toward standard chiller economics; the durable value is in the services annuity (~1/3 of revenue).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\nStep 4-1/2. Scenarios (probabilities sum to 100%; base is the modal case)\n- BEAR (25%): AI-capex digestion; applied bookings normalize from +130% to +10%; residential stays weak; 10Y >5.25%. Revenue growth fades to 3% by 2029; OPM peaks at 19%; WACC 9.5%. IV \u2248 $230/share [Estimate].\n- BASE (50%): As Section 3. Revenue $34.8B by 2032; OPM 21%; WACC 8.75%. IV \u2248 $310\u2013330/share \u2192 $312 [Estimate].\n- BULL (25%): TT captures ~15\u201320% of the 27%-CAGR liquid-cooling TAM; organic growth 11\u201312% through 2028; services grows low-teens; margins 21.5%; WACC 8.5%. IV \u2248 $350\u2013360/share [Estimate]. (Analyst mean PT $525 requires an additional rerating to ~35x \u2014 beyond my bull's DCF.)\n\nStep 4-3. Bayesian update\n- Prior (base rate of mega-cap industrial sustaining >8% growth for a decade): ~35% [Assumption].\n- Evidence: backlog +70%, bookings +37%, guidance raised twice, applied +130% \u00d74 quarters [Actual].\n- Posterior: ~50% [Estimate]. My base+bull = 75% \u2014 at the 1.5x base-rate cap; the delta is justified only by the backlog/services/regulatory evidence above. Flag accepted.\n\nStep 4-4/5. Expected value and margin of safety\nExpected value = 0.25\u00d7230 + 0.50\u00d7312 + 0.25\u00d7350 = $301\n50% CI: $285\u2013$345 | 80% CI: $230\u2013$400\nMargin of safety (base vs T0): (312 \u2212 453.43)/312 = \u221245.3% \u2014 NONE (negative)\nMargin of safety (EV vs T0): (301 \u2212 453.43)/301 = \u221250.6% \u2014 NONE\n3-year scenario target: Bear $230 / Base $345 / Bull $480; E[P\u2083] = $350 \u2192 expected 3-yr CAGR = (350/453)^(1/3) \u2212 1 = \u22128.2%/yr [Arithmetic].\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4.5 \u2014 TIME-HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\n- Market-implied horizon: at 29.7x FY26E and ~26x FY27E, the market is capitalizing ~6\u20138 years of high-teens EPS growth. The market horizon \u2248 6\u20138 years.\n- My horizon: 3\u20135 years (the period over which backlog and guidance provide visibility).\n- Spread = \u22121 to \u22123 years (negative arbitrage): at this price, the market's horizon is LONGER than any evidence base I can verify. No arbitrage opportunity in my favor.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 5 \u2014 CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\nStep 5-1. Conviction score (0\u20133 each; total /15)\n1. Business quality: 3 (fortress moat, ROIC 25%+, Piotroski 8/8, OCF/NI TTM 133%)\n2. Valuation attractiveness: 1 (MoS \u