6) |\n\n## 1.5-2. Justification for deviation from base rate (required)\n\n1. **Backlog structure**: $12.1B record backlog, ~$6B slated for 2027+, providing unprecedented near-term visibility [Actual \u2014 Q2'26 call].\n2. **Data-center AI cooling is a structural demand shift**, not just a cycle: Applied bookings +130% 4 consecutive quarters; hyperscaler capex forecast ~$1T by 2027 [Estimate \u2014 Moody's].\n3. **Services mix**: 1/3 of revenue growing low-teens CAGR since 2020; recurring revenue creates visibility that typical industrials lack [Actual \u2014 Q2'26 call].\n\n## 1.5-3. Base-rate shock check\n\n- My base case assumes 5-year revenue CAGR of ~6.6% (FY26-FY30), which exceeds the ~2-3% industrial base rate by **~2.2x**.\n- **RED FLAG triggered.** Note this explicitly: the forecast already embeds above-base-rate performance. If the data-center cycle breaks, the downward scenario is severe (20% scenario, $202/share bear case).\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\n## 2-1. Business Understanding (3 sentences)\n\nTrane Technologies designs, manufactures, sells, and services commercial and residential HVAC equipment (chillers, rooftops, heat pumps, controls) primarily in the Americas, EMEA, and Asia-Pacific. The company generates ~1/3 of revenue from high-margin recurring services (maintenance, parts, controls) on its installed base of millions of units. TT is currently experiencing a demand super-cycle in data-center liquid and air-cooled thermal management, with bookings +37% and a record backlog of $12.1B.\n\n**Understandability: HIGH**\n\n## 2-2. Moat Assessment + Dynamics\n\n| Moat type | Score (0-3) | Direction | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (brand) | 3 | Widening | Trane is a 100+ yr premium brand; innovation leadership in efficient chillers |\n| Switching costs | 3 | Stable | Installed base lock-in; proprietary controls and service contracts |\n| Network effects | 0 | \u2014 | Not material |\n| Cost advantage | 1 | Stable | Scale benefits but not lowest-cost |\n| Economies of scale | 2 | Widening | 4x applied capacity expansion in 3 years [Actual \u2014 Q2'26] |\n| Regulatory barriers | 2 | Widening | Energy efficiency mandates (SEER, building codes) favor premium equipment |\n| Data moat | 1 | Widening | Agentic AI / connected building data improves efficiency analytics |\n\n**Total: 12/21 \u2014 WIDE moat** | **Direction: Widening**\n\n## 2-3. Lynch Classification\n\n**Fast Grower with Stalwart characteristics** (growth >15% EPS in 2026, but services give durability). Checkpoints are:\n- Balance sheet: Net debt/EBITDA ~0.8x \u2192 PASS\n- FCF generation: $2.75B after SBC \u2192 PASS\n- Defensible growth: AI cooling + services \u2192 PASS\n- Valuation (Lynch would say PEG >1.5-2.0 is interesting but his rule of thumb fails here): PEG trailing 1.95 \u2192 borderline FAIL (premium)\n\n## 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard\n\n| Metric | Value | Tag |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue CAGR (5yr trailing) | 11.3% | [Actual \u2014 pack] |\n| Operating margin (latest FY) | 18.6% | [Actual \u2014 arithmetic] |\n| Operating margin (median 12yr) | 12.5% | [Actual \u2014 pack] |\n| ROE (vendor) | 36.6% | [Aggregator] |\n| ROIC (adjusted, est.) | ~19% | [Estimate \u2014 Layer 2.5] |\n| FCF margin | 13.3% (FY25) | [Estimate \u2014 /revenue] |\n| FCF conversion (FCF/OCF) | 88.1% | [Estimate \u2014 /OCF] |\n| Net debt / EBITDA | ~0.85x | [Estimate] |\n| Net cash position | Negative (net debt ~$3.7B incl. leases) | [Estimate] |\n| Interest coverage | >18x | [Estimate \u2014 $3.97B op inc / $0.227B int] |\n\n**Judgment: EXCELLENT**\n\n## 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard (0-15)\n\n| Criteria | Score (0-3) | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Buyback efficiency | 3 | $840M YTD 2026, $3.8B remaining; share count shrinking ~1.5%/yr [Actual \u2014 Q2'26 call + pack] |\n| M&A track record | 2 | Stellar ($500M rev target 2026) early days; history of disciplined tuck-ins |\n| Capex efficiency | 3 | 2-3% of revenue = asset-light; returns >20% on incremental capex |\n| Mix rationality | 3 | Balance of growth capex + buyback + 12% dividend increase |\n| Guidance accuracy | 3 | Raised FY26 guidance twice; consistent beat-and-raise pattern |\n\n**Subtotal: 14/15**\n**Insider modifier**: 0.27% insider ownership, no notable net insider buying \u2192 +0\n\n**Capital Allocation Score: 14/15 \u2014 EXCELLENT**\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\n## 2.5-1. R&D Capitalization\n\n- R&D is not separately disclosed in the pack. Estimated at ~2% of revenue (~$430M FY25) [Assumption].\n- Capitalizing 50% over 4-year life: adjusted Op Income \u2248 $3.967B + ~$108M R&D add-back \u2248 **$4.08B** (steady state, amortization \u2248 addition).\n\n## 2.5-2. SBC Treatment\n\n| Year | SBC | Effect on Adj. FCF |\n|---|---|---|\n| FY2024 | $0.083B | FCF adj. = $2.807B - $0.083B = $2.724B |\n| FY2025 | $0.087B | FCF adj. = $2.837B - $0.087B = $2.750B |\n\n## 2.5-3. Operating Lease Capitalization\n\n- Operating leases: $0.825B (FY2025) [Actual \u2014 pack]\n- Capitalized at 8x: ~$6.6B notional, but **present value** of lease payments (what enters debt properly) is closer to $1.5-2.5B for this scale [Assumption]. I will use **$1.7B** in adjusted debt to avoid 8x overstatement.\n\n## 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (Buffett-style, FY2025)\n\n```\nNet income                  $2.919B\n+ D&A                       $0.376B\n- Maintenance capex (~60% of $0.383B)  -$0.230B\n- Change in working capital -$0.307B\n- SBC (real expense)        -$0.087B\n= Owner earnings            $2.671B  [Estimate]\n```\nOwner earnings per share: $2.671B / 224.9M = **$11.88** (vs. reported EPS $12.98 \u2014 9% gap from SBC and WC drag).\n\n## 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC\n\n```\nAdjusted NOPAT (FY25): ~$2.34B (op income 4.08 \u00d7 (1-0.26 tax est.))\nAdjusted invested capital: Equity $8.601B + Net debt $3.7B \u2248 $12.3B\nAdjusted ROIC: ~19%  [Estimate]\n```\nGAAP ROIC \u2248 17-18%. Gap < 5%p \u2014 no distortion.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3. VALUATION \u2014 ENGINE 1 (Quality Compounder DCF + Multiples)\n\n## 3-1. Discount Rate Derivation\n\n| Input | Value | Source/Tag |\n|---|---|---|\n| Risk-free rate (10Y) | 4.70% | [Actual \u2014 FRED, 8/24/26] |\n| Equity risk premium | 4.0% | [Assumption \u2014 Damodaran-style long-run; conservative at current high rates] |\n| Beta | 1.205 | [Aggregator] |\n| Cost of equity | 4.70% + 1.205 \u00d7 4.0% = **9.52%** | [Estimate] |\n| Cost of debt (pre-tax) | ~5.0% | [Assumption \u2014 A-rated industrial, ~30bps over 10Y] |\n| Tax rate | 26% | [Assumption \u2014 GAAP eff. tax, but tax credits may lower; note 22-24% possible] |\n| After-tax cost of debt | 3.70% | [Estimate] |\n| Debt weight (EV) | 3.6% | [Estimate] |\n| **WACC** | **~9.3%** | [Estimate \u2014 rounded 9.4% for simplicity] |\n\n## 3-2. Explicit FCF Forecast (FY2026A-E through FY2030E)\n\n| Line ($B) | FY2025A | FY2026E | FY2027E | FY2028E | FY2029E | FY2030E |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue | 21.32 | 23.10 | 25.05 | 26.60 | 28.10 | 29.50 |\n| Growth % | \u2014 | 8.3% | 8.4% | 6.2% | 5.6% | 5.0% |\n| Operating margin | 18.6% | 18.8% | 19.2% | 19.5% | 19.7% | 19.9% |\n| Operating income | 3.97 | 4.34 | 4.81 | 5.19 | 5.54 | 5.87 |\n| Net income (GAAP est.) | 2.92 | 3.25 | 3.62 | 3.92 | 4.18 | 4.43 |\n| + D&A | 0.38 | 0.42 | 0.45 | 0.48 | 0.51 | 0.54 |\n| - \u0394WC | 0.31 | 0.25 | 0.30 | 0.30 | 0.30 | 0.30 |\n| - Capex (2.5% rev) | 0.38 | 0.58 | 0.63 | 0.67 | 0.70 | 0.74 |\n| - SBC | 0.09 | 0.10 | 0.11 | 0.12 | 0.13 | 0.14 |\n| **FCF** | **2.84** | **3.20** | **3.60** | **3.95** | **4.28** | **4.65** |\n| FCF/share | \u2014 | 14.68 | 16.67 | 18.46 | 20.19 | 22.14 |\n\n**Forecast source labels**:\n- FY2026 revenue growth: [Actual guidance ~9% organic \u2192 8.3% reported using H1 actuals ($11.32B) + Q3 guidance ~10% + Q4 est. +5.5%] [Estimate]\n- FY2027: [Estimate \u2014 backlog visibility $6B into 2027 + services low-teens + residential recovery; but fading. This is ABOVE industrial base rate \u2014 deliberate, justified in 1.5-2]\n- FY2028-2030: [Assumption \u2014 linear fade to GDP+1%; **no company guidance exists beyond 2026** \u2014 flagged per framework]\n- Margins: [Assumption \u2014 services mix + price-cost; could be upset by EMEA margin drag (Middle East -30% revenue)]\n\n## 3-3. Terminal Value\n\n```\nTerminal FCF = $4.65B \u00d7 1.03 = $4.79B\nTerminal growth = 3.0% [Assumption \u2014 long-run nominal GDP, <3.5% discount]\nWACC = 9.4%\nTerminal Value = $4.79B / (9.4% - 3.0%) = $74.8B [Estimate]\n```\n\n## 3-4. DCF Calculation\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| PV FCFs 2026-2030 (9.4%) | $13.63B |\n| PV Terminal Value | $47.75B |\n| EV | $61.38B |\n| Less: Net debt (LT debt 3.92 + current portion 0.69 + leases 1.7 - cash 1.76) | -$4.55B |\n| Equity value | $56.83B |\n| Per share (220.3M shares) | **$258** |\n\n**Sensitivity \u2014 WACC \u00d7 Terminal growth:**\n\n| WACC | g=2.5% | g=3.0% | g=3.5% |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 8.4% | $320 | $350 | $389 |\n| 9.4% | $260 | $281 | $309 |\n| 10.4% | $214 | $229 | $248 |\n\n## 3-5. Multiples Cross-Check\n\n| Metric | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Trailing P/E (TTM NI $2.949B, 220.3M sh) | 33.8x |\n| Forward P/E (FY26 adj EPS $15.25) | 29.7x |\n| EV/EBITDA (estimated FY26) | ~22x |\n| P/FCF (FY25 FCF $2.84B) | 35x |\n| 10-yr median P/E (estimated) | ~25x [Estimate \u2014 not directly in pack] |\n| Peer set (Carrier ~28x fwd, JCI ~20x, Daikin ~25x) | TT is 10-20% above peer median [Estimate \u2014 web-derived] |\n\n**Multiples indicate**: TT is priced at a ~20% premium to quality peers and a ~30% premium to its own history.\n\n## 3-6. Reverse DCF \u2014 What does $453.43 imply?\n\nTo justify $453.43 at 9.4% WACC and 3% terminal growth, the market is embedding:\n- FY2027 revenue: $26.5B (+15%, above guidance momentum)\n- FY2030 revenue: $42B (7-year CAGR ~12%)\n- FY2030 FCF margin: 17% \u2192 FCF \u2248 $7.1B\n- OR a lower required return (~7.5-8.0%) \u2014 implying the market treats TT as a bond-proxy quality compounder.\n\n**Verdict: FEASIBLE only in the bull case; EXCESSIVE relative to base-rate evidence.**\n\n## 3-7. Tornado Sensitivity \u2014 Top 3 Variables\n\n1. **FY2027-29 revenue growth (\u00b14%)**: \u00b1$65/share impact \u2014 **Thesis KPI: Bookings and backlog conversion**\n2. **WACC (\u00b1100bps)**: \u00b1$50/share \u2014 **Thesis KPI: 10Y Treasury and beta**\n3. **Terminal margin (\u00b1150bps)**: \u00b1$35/share \u2014 **Thesis KPI: Services mix / price-cost**\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\n## 3.5-1. Competitive Response\n\nCarrier, JCI, Daikin, Mitsubishi, and Vertiv are all expanding data-center cooling capacity. If TT's success continues, expect:\n- **Price competition** in liquid cooling (-2 impact): Vertiv has strong position, LiquidStack acquisition helps TT.\n- **Capacity response** (-1): Competitors will also add 2-3x capacity; segment won't stay supply-constrained.\n- **Net effect: -2** (mild-to-moderate headwind after 2027)\n\n## 3.5-2. Reflexivity Check (Soros)\n\n- **Positive**: TT's high P/E gives it cheap acquisition currency (Stellar), high SBC attracts AI talent, backlog reinforces customer trust.\n- **Negative**: If the stock corrects 30%, SBC becomes less valuable, customers may delay decisions, and the \"quality premium\" narrative could reverse.\n- **Net: Positive while the stock is high \u2014 but this cuts both ways on the downside. High crowding makes the reflexivity risk asymmetric.**\n\n## 3.5-3. Customer Behavior Evolution\n\n- Early adopters: Hyperscalers (2019-2023) \u2192 Mainstream: Colocation and enterprise data centers (2024-2026) \u2192 Late: Commercial buildings with AI-driven efficiency.\n- LTV/CAC: TT's service attach rate on new chillers is high; services CVaR is stable.\n- Risk: Hyperscalers are sophisticated buyers with pricing power; as they standardize designs, they may commoditize the hardware and squeeze margins (already visible in the \"four $100M+ orders\" \u2014 these are large, competitive bids).\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\n## 4-1/4-2. Scenario Composition\n\n| Scenario | Probability | 2030 EPS | Terminal P/E | 2030 Price | PV@9.4% to T0 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Bear** \u2014 data center cycle breaks; recession 2027-28; margin compression | 20% | $16.50 | 16x | $264 | **$168** |\n| **Base** \u2014 growth fades to GDP+2%; services carry; book-to-bill normalizes | 50% | $22.00 | 24x | $528 | **$337** |\n| **Bull** \u2014 AI cooling supercycle extends; applied + data center remain >30% growth; margin expands to 21% | 30% | $27.50 | 27x | $743 | **$474** |\n\n**Expected Price T0** = 0.20\u00d7168 + 0.50\u00d7337 + 0.30\u00d7474 = **$352** [Estimate]\n\n## 4-3. Bayesian Update\n\n- Prior from base rates (Sec 1.5): Probability of sustained double-digit EPS growth: 25-30%\n- Update trigger: Q2'26 bookings +37%, backlog +70%, guidance raised \u2192 **Likelihood ratio \u2248 2.5x**\n- **Posterior probability of bull-like growth scenario: ~30%** (as assigned above). Note: the posterior is already incorporated \u2014 it is not the 50-60% a momentum investor might assume.\n\n## 4-4. Margin of Safety\n\n| Measure | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Scenario-weighted expected price | $352 |\n| DCF point (conservative) | $258 |\n| Blended IV (60% scenario + 40% DCF) | **$390** |\n| Margin of safety vs $453.43 | **-16.3%** |\n| Judgment | **NONE \u2014 overvalued** |\n\n## 4-5. Confidence Intervals\n\n- 50% CI: $350\u2013$430\n- 80% CI: $270\u2013$520\n- The distribution is wide because the data-center cycle duration is the dominant unknown. [Unconfirmed: no company guidance beyond 2026 for data-center growth]\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\n**Market-implied horizon**: At $453.43 with FY26 EPS $15.25, the market is pricing **~6-7 years of flawless compounding** (discounted present value of EPS through 2032-33 at a 10% required return).\n\n**Your/our horizon**: 3-5 years (default).\n\n**Spread = 2-3 years** \u2014 the market is looking