Stock is 10% off highs |\n| **Crowding Risk Score** | **13/15** | Institutions 92%, momentum factor crowding, data-center/AI narrative is consensus long |\n\n**Impact**: Position size cap -1% (if it were otherwise appropriate). Crowding raises the probability of a violent 20-30% drawdown if AI-capex data disappoints even mildly.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\n- TT is a **large-cap industrial with 1.2 beta** and high crowding. Its role in a portfolio: cyclical growth, data-center/AI infrastructure exposure, quality compounder ballast.\n- Correlation risk: High correlation with other AI-infrastructure names (Vertiv, Eaton, GE Vernova) \u2014 potential simultaneous drawdown.\n- **Fit: Good company, wrong price.** A 0% position at T0 is appropriate; if the pullback to $380 occurs, an initial 3% position could be justified for portfolios underweight industrials/AI cooling.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\n```\nEdge = (IV - Price) / Price = ($390 - $453.43) / $453.43 = -13.9%\nOdds = upside/downside in scenarios \u2248 $474/$168 \u2248 2.82\nKelly fraction = Edge / Odds = -13.9% / 2.82 = -4.9%\n```\n\n**Negative Kelly \u2192 position size = 0.** The market is offering negative expected value at T0.\n\n**Re-check (Rule 13 of framework)**: Layer 5 says 0% (valuation override), Layer 6.5 says 0%. Gap = 0% \u2192 consistent.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\n## 7-1. Upside Catalysts\n\n1. **Hyperscaler capex re-acceleration**: If 2027 AI capex exceeds $1.2T (consensus ~$1T), Applied orders could inflect again.\n2. **Residential recovery**: Bookings were up high-20s% in Q2'26, the ACT projects a market bottom in H1'26 and recovery in H2. A US housing recovery could add 2-3% to revenue growth.\n3. **Additional capacity expansion announcements** signalling confidence.\n4. **Further service penetration** \u2014 services growing low-teens could push mix above 35% of revenue by 2028, structurally raising the margin ceiling.\n5. **Continued share repurchases**: $3.8B remaining \u2014 ~3.8% of market cap available to be retired.\n\n## 7-2. Downside Risks\n\n| Risk | Probability | Impact |\n|---|---|---|\n| Data-center capex pause / AI winter | 20-25% | -30% revenue growth in Applied; 25-30% stock drawdown |\n| Commercial construction downturn (rates 4.7% persist) | 30% | -10-15% earnings |\n| Valuation de-rating even on good fundamentals | 50% (within 2y) | Multiple compression from 30x to 22x = -27% |\n| EMEA/China deterioration | 40% | Margin drag persists |\n| Competition (Vertiv, Carrier, JCI) intensifies | 50% | Price/margin pressure 2028+ |\n| Crowding unwind | 35% | Fast 20%+ drawdown |\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\n- **Common bear trigger across portfolio**: A global AI capex disappointment would hit TT, VRT, ETN, GEV simultaneously. If the portfolio holds multiple AI-infrastructure names, the effective tail exposure is concentrated.\n- **Tail correlation**: Historically ~0.6-0.8 during stress events (2020, 2022) for same-factor industrials. Assuming 0.7 for a drawdown scenario:\n  - CVaR estimate (95%): If TT drops 35% in a severe scenario, portfolio impact is not diversified away \u2014 it compounds with other AI names.\n- **Recommendation**: If a portfolio owns TT at T0, limit combined AI-infrastructure factor weight to <20%.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 8. MONITORING RULES\n\n## Thesis-Integrity Criteria (Trigger any \u2192 re-evaluate)\n\n| KPI | Threshold | Current | Frequency |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Enterprise organic bookings | \u226515% YoY | +37% | Quarterly |\n| Book-to-bill | \u22651.0x | >1.2x | Quarterly |\n| Americas Commercial Applied orders | \u2265+30% | +130% | Quarterly |\n| Services organic growth | \u226510% | Low-teens | Quarterly |\n| Adjusted operating margin | \u226518.5% | ~19% (est.) | Quarterly |\n| FCF conversion | \u226585% of NI | ~88% [Arithmetic] | Annually |\n| Backlog exiting FY26 | \u2265$8B | $12.1B | Quarterly |\n\n## Watch items\n- Any quarter where **bookings growth <10%** \u2192 data-center cycle rollover warning.\n- Any guidance **miss** (first in 3 years) \u2192 management credibility break, immediate re-evaluation.\n- **Fed re-tightening** \u2192 construction demand at risk.\n- **Hyperscaler CFO commentary** on capex discipline \u2192 lead indicator with 2-quarter lag.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 9. RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL\n\n## 9-1. Short Thesis (from the same data)\n\n1. **Valuation is a bubble**: 30x forward EPS for a company growing ~9% organically and 15% EPS \u2014 the PEG is already 2x. Every 1pt of multiple compression wipes ~$16/share.\n2. **Data center capex is a front-loaded investment cycle**: $301B in H1'26 across four hyperscalers. The marginal dollar of AI revenue is unproven; if AI monetization disappoints, capex gets cut by 20-30% within 2 quarters, hitting TT's Applied orders.\n3. **Commercial HVAC is inherently cyclical**: TT's own 12-year history shows operating margin swinging between 11-19%. The current mid-to-high 18% is peak-cycle margin.\n4. **Market share is small (~3%)**: Competition from Carrier (28x fwd), JCI, Daikin, Vertiv is intense \u2014 TT has no structural shortage in cooling capacity, just timing.\n5. **The company is not priced for any bad news**: At $453, even a \"good but not perfect\" year (EPS $14.75 vs $15.25 guidance) would send the stock to $400 (-12%).\n6. **Crowding is extreme**: 92% institutional ownership; the stock is a \"quality compounder\" consensus favorite \u2014 these unwind violently when they break.\n7. **EMEA exposure and FX risk are underappreciated**: Middle East revenue -30% (conflict), China weakness, and a strong dollar would add EPS headwinds not in the guidance.\n\n## 9-2. Logic Combat\n\n| Dimension | Long thesis strength | Short thesis strength |\n|---|---|---|\n| Data-centre growth | High (backlog proves near-term) | Medium (cycle duration unknown) |\n| Business quality/moat | Very High | Medium (cyclical margin at peak) |\n| Valuation | Low (30x forward) | High (PEG >2, FCF yield < rf) |\n| Evidence quality | High (filing-based) | High (same filings, different interpretation) |\n\n**Gap: 1.5/10 \u2192 Logic gap is narrow enough to warrant conviction downgrade from Buy to Neutral/Avoid.**\n\n## 9-3. Adversarial Data\n\nNo Hindenburg-style active short-seller report was found. The primary adversarial sources are:\n- Seeking Alpha analysis citing \"Nvidia woes and bearish tendencies outweigh strength\" [Estimate].\n- Sell-side low target of $422 \u2014 7% below current price [Actual \u2014 aggregator].\n- **No credible accounting-red-flag claims found.** [Unconfirmed \u2014 no adversarial forensic report]\n\n## 9-4. Killed Arguments\n\n| Argument I dismissed | Conditions that would make it valid |\n|---|---|\n| \"Hyperscaler capex will definitely keep growing\" | If AI monetization stalls, RPOs plateau, or financing costs for DC projects spike |\n| \"TT deserves a structural premium for quality\" | Only with evidence of >20% ROIC sustainability through a full cycle AND a 3%+ premium to GDP growth |\n| \"Margins can keep rising to 22-23%\" | Only if services grows to 40%+ of revenue while applied normalizes (not currently guided) |\n| \"The stock is expensive but always is\" | This has been true for 2 years \u2014 but a violation occurs when growth misses by more than 2pts OR backlog rolls over |\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS\n\n## A. Methodology Audit\n- [PASS] Engine 1 selected for Quality Compounder; cross-checked with scenario-DCF; not solely DCF-based.\n- [PASS] Explicit 5-year forecast shown with per-year revenue, margins, capex, D&A, and WC.\n- [PASS] Base Rate check included with deviation justification.\n\n## B. Valuation Audit\n- [PASS] WACC inputs traceable: Rf 4.70% [Actual], Beta 1.205 [Aggregator], ERP 4.0% [Assumption \u2014 stated].\n- [PASS] Terminal growth 3.0% \u2264 nominal GDP.\n- [PASS] Scenario probabilities sum to 100%, base case is highest probability.\n- [WARN] DCF point ($258) vs sc